 - Low. The change is one private function and one constant in `offset.rs`. The pinning test is `crates/motion/tests/turning_offset_spacing.rs`.
 - Changing `MIN_GAP_SHARE` changes the chord bound. Keep the bound at `2 * share`, or update the tolerance claim in this entry.
 - Known limit: at a very small user tolerance, for example 0.001 mm, the gap is below the posted resolution. Two rounded blocks can then still be equal. This is rare and it costs no position.
+
+**Review fix (PR #2897).** The catalog case `turning/cone-thread` reached main after this branch was cut. Its committed `.nc` had the repeated blocks. The branch merges main and rebuilds the case with `cargo xtask catalog --case turning/cone-thread`. The rebuild removes 465 `G01` lines and adds none. The runs of same-X `G01` blocks go from 509 to 82. The case README drops the limit line for #2882. The taper and thread blocks that the README names stay the same.
diff --git a/examples/catalog/turning/cone-thread/README.md b/examples/catalog/turning/cone-thread/README.md
index 9c0ea1562..adbab514a 100644
--- a/examples/catalog/turning/cone-thread/README.md
+++ b/examples/catalog/turning/cone-thread/README.md
@@ -52,7 +52,6 @@ Each limit has its own issue. The case can become simpler when they are fixed.
 - **Two models:** Profile Finish reads the thread valleys and the neck step of the finished part as rest, and blocks the post ([#2880](https://github.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/issues/2880)).
 - **G32 passes, not the G76 cycle:** a thread pass cannot start in front of the end face ([#2879](https://github.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/issues/2879)), so a G76 cycle would start inside the part ([#2878](https://github.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/issues/2878)). The G32 passes plunge at feed.
 - **A sharp shoulder:** a fillet at the shoulder sends the profile rides far out of the bar ([#2881](https://github.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/issues/2881)).
-- **Repeated blocks:** the profile rides post runs of identical `G01` blocks ([#2882](https://github.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/issues/2882)).
 - **The verify verdict** says that the lathe stock simulation does not cut threads. The thread is judged by its own operation verdict.
 - **The holders are not judged:** the tools have no modelled body.
 
